{
  "dom_id": "step-q208823",
  "name": "Question 9",
  "problem": "Consider the following system of equations.\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-2x+y=6 \\\\ 3x+4y=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe variable $y$ can be eliminated from the system using the following two steps:\n\n1\\. Multiply the first equation by $-4$ and obtain\n\n  $$\n  \\begin{aligned}8x-4y=-24.\\end{aligned}\n  $$\n\n2\\. Add the multiplied equation to the second equation to get\n\n$\\,$\n{{field-1}}$=-22.$\n\n$\\,$\nHence, the solution to the system of equations is $($\n{{field-2}}$\\,,$\n{{field-3}}\n$).$",
  "worked_solution": "Let's follow the steps provided:\n\n**Step 1**: Multiplying the first equation by $-4$ yields the following equivalent system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}(\\,-2x+y=6\\,)\\,\\times (-4) \\\\ 3x+4y=2\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}8x-4y=-24 \\\\ 3x+4y=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we add these two equations together, *eliminate* the variable $y,$ and solve the resulting equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8x-\\cancel{4y} & =-24 \\\\ 3x+\\cancel{4y} & =2 \\\\ (8x+3x) & =(-24+2) \\\\ 11x & =-22\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $x=-2.$ However, we still need to find $y.$ To do this, we substitute $x=-2$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2x+y & =6 \\\\ -2(-2)+y & =6 \\\\ 4+y & =6 \\\\ y & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $x=-2,\\,y=2,$ which can be expressed as $(-2,2).$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
